{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names","id":1300766,"title":"Looking for a book that had misleading tribe names","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-03-28T21:53:53-07:00","id":1300766,"title":"Looking for a book that had misleading tribe names","content":"There was a book I read that had a nonwerewolf perspective. In one section, they mentioned something about the lupine tribes in the area, but got them jumbled (the way they should). They thought that there were two or three tribes and mixed together parts of the names - one set that lived in the city that was a jumble of glass walkers and bone gnawer, and another ruling group more or less called the Silverfang Shadowlords - basically a game of telephone with the tribenames. <br /> <br /> I think it may have been a regional book (i.e. X by night), and I think it was set in Europe (I think more eastern - between Germany and Russia, inclusicely) , and think it was a vampire book, but may be wrong on any and all of the counts.<br /> <br /> Does anyone know the book I am talking about? I want to find that exact example as a demonstration of a point to fellow players about the confusion of information from outside hostile sources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T23:19:56-07:00","id":1300773,"title":"","content":"There is an NPC in one of the books, can't find who or what book. He's pieced together enough of the lore as an intelligence analyst to know bit and pieces. He messes up the names of some things, like tribes, relates things that aren't related and the like. There's a picture of his conspiracy board that links things together in that book. I want to say it's a Pentex related book, but the only thing I'm certain of is that it exists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16927-illithid","name":"Illithid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar16927_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T00:41:07-07:00","id":1300778,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Illithid</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1300773#post1300773\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is an NPC in one of the books, can't find who or what book. He's pieced together enough of the lore as an intelligence analyst to know bit and pieces. He messes up the names of some things, like tribes, relates things that aren't related and the like. There's a picture of his conspiracy board that links things together in that book. I want to say it's a Pentex related book, but the only thing I'm certain of is that it exists.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's in Project Twilight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T05:44:36-07:00","id":1300807,"title":"","content":"There is also a really funny Tzimisce section in revised clanbookwhere the Tzimi ponders WTF is up with garou being BORN infertile and deformed. It includes messed up tribenames, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T11:23:10-07:00","id":1300885,"title":"","content":"Milwaukee by Night also has some 'tribes' IIRC.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T21:57:00-07:00","id":1301030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1300807#post1300807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is also a really funny Tzimisce section in revised clanbookwhere the Tzimi ponders WTF is up with garou being BORN infertile and deformed. It includes messed up tribenames, too.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> THAT'S the one I remembered. Glassgnawers, Black-Star Walkers, and the Shadowed Lord clan. Thank you! I'll check out the otherse, though... may be helpful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-01T16:45:31-07:00","id":1301745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1300885#post1300885\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Milwaukee by Night also has some 'tribes' IIRC.</div> </div> </div> </div>So found a copy of this and would like to thank you for the reference. It is less &quot;misleading tribe&quot; and more &quot;odd descriptions of packs that may be more akin to tribes&quot; but it was actually kind of useful as a study for this project. Also, the two &quot;werewolf disciplines&quot; in the book were actually fairly helpful to study.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-01T19:12:29-07:00","id":1301783,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EndlessKng</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1301745#post1301745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So found a copy of this and would like to thank you for the reference. It is less &quot;misleading tribe&quot; and more &quot;odd descriptions of packs that may be more akin to tribes&quot; but it was actually kind of useful as a study for this project. Also, the two &quot;werewolf disciplines&quot; in the book were actually fairly helpful to study.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC, Milwaukee by Night was written around the same time that the concept for Werewolf was being hammered out, so it is this interesting window into how that game developed. (Hunters Hunted is also interesting in its presentation of a lupine and a mage.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-06T11:18:09-07:00","id":1303133,"title":"","content":"In the vampire chronicles that I ST, I don't have my vampire NPCs who know some things about Lupines refer to tribes by &quot;name&quot;, but they know enough that there are &quot;cults&quot; who worship certain nature spirits like Thunder, Stag, Boar, Wolf, etc. Tribe and pack totems get all mixed up, and those vampires often interpret the spirits through their own understanding so they use Cronus instead of Grandfather Thunder, or Pan instead of Stag, etc.<br /> <br /> I feel that is somehow a more &quot;realistic&quot; depiction to portray how vampires can understand Lupines partially, but remain ignorant over all. Mangling names just doesn't do that for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 04-15-2019, 09:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-06T13:16:15-07:00","id":1303166,"title":"","content":"I like that. It really adds to the civilized urban vampire stereotype of lupines being these primitive barbaric savages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-06T18:28:38-07:00","id":1303226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1303133#post1303133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the vampire chronicles that I ST, I don't have my vampire NPCs who know some things about Lupines refer to tribes by &quot;name&quot;, but they know enough that there are &quot;cults&quot; who worship certain nature spirits like Thunder, Stag, Boar, Wolf, etc. Tribe and pack totems get all mixed up, and those vampires often interpret the spirits through their own understanding so they use Chronus instead of Grandfather Thunder, or Pan instead of Stag, etc.<br /> <br /> I feel that is somehow a more &quot;realistic&quot; depiction to portray how vampires can understand Lupines partially, but remain ignorant over all. Mangling names just doesn't do that for me.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So on one hand it sort of makes a certain degree of sense in that case. The Tzimisce are one of the ones most likely to have any knowledge - between their lorekeeping, the presence of the Gangrel and Nosferatu in the Sabbat to provide some information, and their own battles with the Shadow Lords, it does make sense to me to allow for the mixed-up names. It also suggests something about the alignment of interests that the Tzimisce are able to see - the ones in the states are running into the urban ones more often because they aren't venturing into the wild, but in Europe there is far less space and far more chances to run into Garou, especially for those Tzimisce who try for isolation - and thus more chances to run into mixed-tribe packs and possibly hear pack names more often. It also suggests that the Tzimisce or their allies have had spies able to learn of the Nation but without total success. In a game with a global focus (which Revised bled into at points), having them see bigger patterns incompletely feels like a good balance between knowing more than they should but not enough to function. Also, it served a particular purpose for a proposal I'm making to my national organization on handling supernaturals from one universe as antagonists in another - I had the idea of grouping up tribes (for VTM) and clans (for WTA) into a couple of groups that could share normally-splat-exclusive mechanics freely (that is, allow a character from that &quot;group&quot; to purchase a tribe or clan merit or tribe-exclusive gift), so that things were both familiar and offputting to knowledgeable PCs, without creating truly new mechanics.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I do like this approach as an idea, especially for smaller games. In Milwaukee by Night, as mentioned by No One of Consequence, this is sort of the approach taken. Garou was still being worked on, as he mentioned, so the ideas therein weren't settled upon and may not have even been made canon in Vampire. Instead, the Lupines of Milkaukee were in three packs, identified by their totems. So, it does make sense that perhaps vampires may see the pack and extrapolate further from that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-09T19:23:06-07:00","id":1303862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EndlessKng</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1301745#post1301745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So found a copy of this and would like to thank you for the reference. It is less &quot;misleading tribe&quot; and more &quot;odd descriptions of packs that may be more akin to tribes&quot; but it was actually kind of useful as a study for this project. Also, the two &quot;werewolf disciplines&quot; in the book were actually fairly helpful to study.</div> </div> </div> </div>What details did they have on the &quot;Disciplines&quot; for the werewolves?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16927-illithid","name":"Illithid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar16927_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T02:28:20-07:00","id":1303904,"title":"","content":"Fairly limited in terms of mechanics but still interesting. <br /> <br /> One was called &quot;The Howl.&quot; No differentiated abilities at different levels that I saw even though it had a rating of four (the effects weren't separated out as a whole new discipline making it easy to miss). But basically it was a howl of intimate designed to dishearten or terrify the foe.<br /> <br /> The more interesting one was &quot;Break Boundaries.&quot; That went up to three on that character, and mentioned three discrete effects - escape from bindings or confinement, enter the spirit world, and make others aware of the limitations they put on themselves.<br /> <br /> The rest were standard disciplines - Auspex, physicals, and obfuscate mostly, though there may have been others. <br /> <br /> On a side note I think my favorite ever was actually Diablerie: Mexico. They had lupine minions for the big Methuselah and their only power was Celerity. <br /> But in their favor, it was Celerity *6*.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1407,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","user":"EndlessKng","date":". 04-10-2019, 05:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T11:03:24-07:00","id":1303995,"title":"","content":"Hmm ... I wonder if a 1st ed Vampire 'Clanbook: Lupine' would be at all feasible for the STV.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T16:47:18-07:00","id":1304044,"title":"","content":"It might be. I remember reading the first LARP rules and the lines section mentioned the wyrm... but when I read the section in the first core book, the lines described were absolutely alien compared to the garou we ended up with. I am going 5o see if I can find my 1e Player Guide, too... iirc it had &quot;lupine lore&quot; that may vary greatly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-11T10:11:26-07:00","id":1304228,"title":"","content":"In the Clanbook: Nosferatu (2nd edition) there was a joke about the names of the Werewolf Tribes, in the opinion of the clan of the Lupines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2250-magus","name":"Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-11T21:31:47-07:00","id":1304394,"title":"","content":"Also, going the other way, the Strider revised tribebook section on vampires has a few regional names and beliefs associated with Vampires in lieu of actual clan lore. The Setites are the Cult of Sutekh, the Gangrel are &quot;mulo,&quot; shapeshifters who often dwell among the Roma, the Ravnos are rakshasa, who are &quot;actually&quot; spirits who inhabit dead bodies and make illusions, and the Nosferatu and Ventrue are known to be American sewer dwellers and corporate vampires respectively. In addition, the vampyr of Eastern Europe war with the Shadow Lorda (Tzimisce) and the draugr are ancient foes of the Get (also Gangrel I think? They didn't get much description tbh.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-14T21:42:09-07:00","id":1304989,"title":"","content":"Insomnia leads to inspiration. Though we &quot;know&quot; the most likely members of those groups, I wanted to break down the types to create a &quot;pool&quot; for each, mixing the WoD lore and actual lore from those regions. The idea is to create groups that could work as a &quot;mixed group&quot; to draw from or to blend together to form antagonists that aren't identical to what you had before.<br /> -Cult of Sutekh: Obviously standard Setites and Vipers work well here. However, although the Setites are the primary type, we can branch out to others as well. If not playing hard and fast, the Cult could also incorporate Baali to represent the corruption angle further (using the versions where True Faith is more painful but objects of faith don't necessarily harm them). Lasombra could also fit in - as creatures of shadow, they would feel right at home to the Cult. And, there could be room for any type of necromancer in the Cult as well - Cappadocians would make a lot of sense,actually, either as &quot;young&quot; Cappadocians who simply look pallid or as corpse-like prophets from beyond the reach of Osiris' kingdom. The Nagaraja could also work - perhaps tying them into the Ammut myth, and making Ammut a slave to the Aeons with the Nagaraja as her children. Then again, Setite Sorcery can simulate Necromancy. If designing a &quot;new clan,&quot; you could probably use the basic Setite spread, but have bloodlines to incorporate other possible powers from those mentioned herein<br /> <br /> -The Ghuls: I actually forgot these ones. The Ghuls reportedly seduced their victims; natives to the Arabian peninsula, they took alluring forms. This could be anyone with Obfuscate or Vicissitude, but Presence also could help you become more seductive overall. Envisioning this group as a mix of Toreador, Assamites, Baali, Tzimisce, and Setites who aren't tied to the actual cult. As a regional bloodline or new clan, I'd go with Obfuscate, Presence, and Vicissitude.<br /> <br /> -Mulo: the mention of shifting immediately implies Gangrel, but we know Ravnos were among the Roma's numbers and wandered in their circles. Perhaps the shapeshifting also could be Vicissitude. Regardless, Protean and/or Vicissitude is a must have... possibly in Animalism as well.<br /> <br /> -Draugr: Wotan is canonically a Gangrel, and this makes some sense. However, he also could be of the Noiad bloodline, given Odin's prophetic abilities. Other visionaries could be Malkavians and Cappadocians (the latter of whom would give rise to corpse-like myths from the draugr. Norse-focused Lhiannan could also be involved <br /> <br /> Aaand bed is hitting finally. Goodnight!<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-15T02:19:17-07:00","id":1305007,"title":"","content":"With the exception of the Cult, all of them are real world vampire/undead legends (thou the modern types are mostly Hollywood). It was actually a writers' goal that the book not mention the Setites/Followers of Set or any other vampire group by name. <br /> <br /> Traditional Roma likely consider Garou to also be mulo, as they are shapeshifters with supernatural seduction powers who leave behind &quot;tainted&quot; children.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-15T20:41:10-07:00","id":1305196,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1300766-looking-for-a-book-that-had-misleading-tribe-names?p=1305007#post1305007\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With the exception of the Cult, all of them are real world vampire/undead legends (thou the modern types are mostly Hollywood). It was actually a writers' goal that the book not mention the Setites/Followers of Set or any other vampire group by name.<br /> <br /> Traditional Roma likely consider Garou to also be mulo, as they are shapeshifters with supernatural seduction powers who leave behind &quot;tainted&quot; children.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. I knew they were references to localized vampire beliefs, but I didn't realize that it was a choice to avoid mentioning the groups directly. Honestly, though... I kind of like it as a design choice. It kind of gets at what I was having an issue with when I created this thread - too many people know both games and the NPCs are too obvious. If the werewolves who make hunting vampires their primary mission doesn't know all the truths and mingles them with local traditions, it's possible that no one really knows what the enemies truly are (Except the Bone Gnawers maybe, cause they actually talk to them). So I still kind of like the idea of using them as &quot;groups&quot; of clans to create some... let's say &quot;unique&quot; encounters for PCs without having to marry yourself too closely to the world of Vampire, but while also providing stability for the setting to follow.<br /> <br /> Even so... really good effort on the author's part. I approve! And thanks for the tidbit!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}